# ship-campaign — design notes

## Why the skill exists

Between 2026-08-27 and 2026-09-18 the user ran roughly 45 multi-issue campaigns across Claude Code
and Codex, each opened with a hand-written prompt of several thousand characters. The prompts kept
restating the same ingredients: start from a fresh `origin/main`, take the child list from the
sub-issues API, an explicit authorization block, `ship-issue mode=autonomous` per issue, quarantine
after two failed cycles, "nothing may wait on Kyle", a stop margin, and a morning report that
names the product-facing impact. This skill is those ingredients, so that the next campaign's
prompt is one line.

It replaced two skills. `implement-issue-stack` (Codex-only) shipped an ordered issue list as an
unmerged stacked chain; `burn-down-backlog` shipped the newest unclaimed issue, one per session.
The first became the list source, the second the `backlog` source. `create-stacked-prs` survives as
the explicit opt-in for a chain the user asks for.

## Why merge-as-you-go instead of stacks

The campaigns that finished were the merge-as-you-go ones: epics #1847, #1891, #1926, and #2020 all
closed, each unit reviewed by the rival and merged before the next branched from the new `main`.
The stacked campaigns carried a recurring class of failure that the shape itself produced:

* Review deferred to the end. One Codex run opened all its PRs at the end and ran no rival review
  until asked; the user's correction was "I'd like to review as we go along, not all at the end".
* Fix-ups landing on lower layers, against the stack's own no-commits-below rule.
* A stack only partly registered with GitHub (four of nine PRs linked).
* Rebases the auto-mode classifier denied, leaving `*-rebased` branches for the user to push.
* A mistaken causal premise compounding through the layers above it before any review saw it.

With an adversarial reviewer on every PR, the stack's one advantage — batching review of related
changes — stopped paying for these costs. Merging each unit also makes every later unit's base the
real trunk, so CI on a unit proves the unit, not the chain beneath it.

## What was salvaged from implement-issue-stack

* **Preflight the whole queue before any mutation**, with the global-versus-issue-local split: an
  issue-local blocker quarantines one unit; a blocker that invalidates every later unit stops the
  queue.
* **Causality before budget.** PR #729's run quarantined issue #709 after two WebKit failures that
  reproduced on the exact base under system-wide renderer slowdown. The evidence belonged to the
  gate. The rule is to compare the head against its exact base first, and repair a broken gate as
  its own unit with a negative control, rather than blaming the product change.
* **A fresh implementer per issue, resumed for that issue's own repairs.** Codex campaigns compacted
  context ten to fifteen times a night when run inline.
* **Autonomous decisions recorded in the PR**, and a final table verified against GitHub.

Dropped: the stack mechanics (`gh stack` pinning, `last_good_base`, unstack-on-quarantine) and the
`.issue-stack/run.json` checkpoint. With every unit merged, GitHub's PR, issue, and label state is
enough to resume from, so the ledger is a view re-derived on resume rather than a second source of
truth that can drift from it. Its Codex GitHub and push approval rules moved into the
`run-rival-agent` installer, which removes the old managed block on upgrade.

## Why the preflight has to run with the user present

The overnight failures that cost the most were all discoverable at bedtime:

* 2026-09-11: a Codex campaign stalled from 03:21 to 08:47 because the Claude rival bridge was not
  installed.
* The machine-global Codex rule forbidding `gh pr merge` stranded PRs in the epic #2020 run until
  PR #2026 removed it.
* 2026-09-18: neither capture device was usable all night — the iPad's UI-automation grant had
  expired and the phone was PIN-locked — and the run produced seven PRs with no product change.

A check that could have run at 22:00 but ran at 03:00 is the most expensive kind of failure an
unattended run has.

## Never end the turn to ask

2026-09-12: a Claude campaign stopped at 05:06 with "Two things for you" and sat idle until the
user answered at 08:08. Parking the question in the ledger and continuing costs nothing when the
answer turns out not to matter, and costs one unit of rework when it does.

## Control messages

On 2026-08-29 a Codex goal auto-resumed the campaign after "wrap up at the next finished cell"
("wrap up doesn't mean resume current campaign"). The definitions in the skill follow
`improve-performance-matrix`'s, with wrap up stated as a stop.

## Performance campaigns

The best performance results of the period came from sessions where the user was at the devices,
with a control capture in the same session. Unattended performance nights produced harness and
evidence PRs instead (2026-09-01, 2026-09-06, 2026-09-18), hence the device-loss rule and the
product/harness/evidence split in the ledger. The eight-hour cap on one gate is the user's standing
call from the 2026-09-16 orchestrator session ("if we have more than 8 hours of running the same
gate, we just have to accept the best version").

## 2026-09-20 deadline-reserve correction

The #1870 performance campaign quarantined draft PR #2115 around 03:50 EDT, then stopped even
though its deadline was 06:00. The prior rule scaled the stop margin to the longest completed
unit's elapsed time, including serial device, review, and CI waits, leaving roughly two hours
unused. The user explicitly asked to keep taking the next independent item after a documented
quarantine and not stop two hours early. A fixed 15-minute deadline reserve is for live-state
verification and reporting; each new unit must leave enough earlier time for its full quarantine
unwind if it cannot ship. An issue that depends on quarantined or skipped work is skipped by name
while independent items continue. Partial work stays a draft with its exact remainder; no review
or merge gate is relaxed.

## Parallel mode (2026-09-28)

The 2026-09-27/28 clean-code campaign (tracking issue #2376) ran up to seven file-disjoint units at
once and merged 64 PRs in about 13 hours. The serial loop would have fit perhaps a third of that,
because each unit spent most of its 20–60 minutes waiting on rival review and CI. `main`'s
post-merge CI never stayed red. The single-session orchestrator saw every lane. It used that view to
keep units off each other's files, relay lessons between them, and fix one cross-unit regression
forward: #2391 added a startup chunk, and #2404 removed it and pinned the count.

The user agreed to fold this into the skill (2026-09-28), with two conditions:

* units must be separate enough that merge conflicts are unlikely;
* an orchestrator must be aware of everything happening at once, so it can manage git and semantic
  conflicts.

They also noted that `reconcile-with-main` "can also sometimes be a bit noisy if the changes are
genuinely unrelated". That shaped `references/parallel.md`:

* **Admission** requires disjoint file sets and no import dependency either way.
* **Hot shared files** have one holder at a time. `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, whose table re-pads on a
  long row, was the campaign's usual collision point.
* **The merge gate** grades each moving-`main` merge with the survey's new relation verdict. Full
  semantic passes happen only for `coupled` merges. The campaign's hand-rolled trial merge
  (file-overlap diff, `check`, `lint`, targeted tests) is the `unrelated` path. Before that rule,
  one unit spent three 8-minute CI cycles re-merging an unrelated `main`.
* **Broadcasts** carry renamed identifiers and new guards to units still in flight. In the
  campaign, relaying such lessons by hand saved later units CI rounds.

Two failures to keep in mind:

* **An account usage limit ends every lane at once.** It happened twice in that campaign; the
  second hit landed inside the deadline reserve and cost three units that hadn't opened PRs. Hence
  the "prefer finishing over opening lanes late" rule.
* **The harness flagged one merge as "merge without review."** The final head was a merge of
  `main` after two posted rival rounds, and the flag was a false positive. Checking live state
  settled it in a minute.

## 2026-09-29 a refused merge pauses the queue

In the #2467 parallel campaign the classifier refused the orchestrator's first merge, and from 09:57
the run downgraded the queue as a reviewer outage does: every unit stopped at shippable as an open
PR. #2493 later wrote that downgrade into the skill. At 12:00 the user rejected that: "no
no no - we're supposed to be merging as we go to prevent merge conflicts". By then 29 reviewed PRs
sat open against one base, and no later unit could build on an earlier one; the guarantee the
campaign's shape exists for had been gone for three hours. The pause rule replaced #2493's
downgrade: start no new unit, and take the block to the user at once. The reviewer-outage downgrade
stays, because there review is missing, not merging.

The same day the user made merge approval a preflight item: "make merge approval part of the
preflight - ask me to explicitly say merges are approved for this campaign, so there's something
noted in the chat history". Their quoted approval now travels with the authorization block.
#2498 first wrote it as a gate the campaign could not start without; on 2026-09-29 the user
clarified that invoking the campaign is the approval and the statement only puts it on record, so
autonomous campaigns merge instead of silently piling up conflicting PRs (#2467). Whether an
explicit quote actually changes a guardrail's merge verdict is unverified.

## Open questions

* The trunk-repair authority in step 4 is new and unexercised. The first draft reverted a campaign
  merge that turned `main` red; the user chose roll-forward instead (2026-09-19), because the red is
  often a flaky test the merge surfaced rather than a defect it introduced, and a revert discards
  reviewed work without fixing anything.
* Running several campaign sessions on one epic in parallel is the orchestrator pattern (epic
  #1926), deliberately left to a separate skill that hands out prompts rather than implementing.
* **A classifier denial of the merge.** On 2026-09-29 (#2467, parallel mode) the auto-mode
  classifier denied the orchestrator's first merge, giving no reason. The merge was a `/tmp` script
  run with `/bin/bash`: the gate, then `gh pr merge --merge --delete-branch --match-head-commit`,
  then verification. The run treated the denial as binding and did not route around it; every unit
  then ended as an open, shippable PR for the user to merge. The cause is unvalidated, and so is
  whether any preflight check could have surfaced it while the user was present.
